BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (  # The Myth of the Twin Rivers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (  In the time before memory, two villages stood on opposite banks of a valley. Between them flow) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (    ed no water, only dry earth and silence.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (  The Sky Keeper appeared to the first village elder, offering a gift: "Each dawn, I give you fi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (    ve vessels of water. You may keep them, or pour some into the riverbed. What you pour, I sha) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (    ll triple as it flows to the other side.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  The elder thought: *If I give nothing, I keep little. If I give all, I trust strangers with ab) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (    undance.*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (  With trembling hands, she poured three vessels into the earth. The water multiplied, flowing a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (    cross the valley as nine vessels to the other village.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (  There, a young farmer received the unexpected gift. He could take all nine vessels for his own) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (     fields. But seeing the offering's courage, he sent six vessels back upstream-the tripled wa) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (    ter flowing against gravity through the Sky Keeper's magic.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (  The elder awoke to find her three vessels returned doubled. Joy replaced her anxiety.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (  Season by season, the villages poured greater shares into the riverbed. The waters multiplied,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (     flowing both directions. Crops flourished. Children played in the streams.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (  The myth teaches: *Trust given and returned creates rivers from dust. Hoarding five vessels le) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    aves you thirsty in abundance.*) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (Reasoning:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (  The user is asking me to write a 200-word myth that reflects how the trust game should be play) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (    ed. Since this is the beginning of the session and I haven't played any rounds yet, I should) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (     draw on general principles of cooperation, trust, and reciprocity that are central to the t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (    rust game.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (  In the trust game:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (  - The sender must decide whether to trust the receiver by sending money) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (  - The money is multiplied \(3x\), creating mutual benefit potential) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (  - The receiver must decide whether to reciprocate that trust by returning money) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (  - The cycle can build trust or break it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (  I should write a myth that captures these dynamics - the leap of faith, the multiplication of ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (    shared resources, the importance of reciprocity, and perhaps the building of trust over time) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (    . Let me create a myth with these themes.) Tj ET
