BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (  # The Myth of the Sacred Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (  In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a valley. Between them flowed a Sacr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (    ed Spring with mystical properties: any offering placed in its waters would triple in abunda) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    nce.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (  The villages were led by Sky-Keeper and Earth-Holder, who took turns approaching the Spring. W) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (    hen Sky-Keeper brought grain, the Spring transformed it into three measures. But Earth-Holde) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (    r, who received the bounty, faced a choice: keep all or return a share to Sky-Keeper, knowin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (    g their roles would reverse with the seasons.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (  At first, Earth-Holder kept everything. Sky-Keeper, learning of this betrayal, brought nothing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (     the next season. Both villages suffered.) Tj ET
